Summary: winemac: in host mode, the displays and current mode come from the host
Category: graphics
Kind: feature
Origin: wine-mac-builds
From: dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_host.m, cocoa_display.m, display.c; written for
 wine-mac-builds, implementing displays_get from
 docs/app-embedded-protocol.md.
Source: none published
Upstream: no
Why: With HostWindows (0069) the host shows the program's windows, and it
 may show them on less than the whole screen: Second Chance keeps them
 below a MacBook's notch. Until now the driver still took its monitors from
 NSScreen and its current mode from CGDisplayCopyDisplayMode, so Wine
 thought the screen was the full height, and Second Chance needed a helper
 injected into Wine that changed what those calls return. Now, in host
 mode, the driver asks the host (displays_get) when it enumerates the
 displays: each monitor's rect and work area are the host's display, the
 current mode's size is that display's size (refresh rate and depth stay
 the real mode's), that size is listed among the modes, and setting it
 changes nothing real. A screen the host doesn't report is left out. The
 host's first display must be at the protocol's origin; when it isn't, or
 the host doesn't answer or answers unsupported, the driver uses the real
 displays, and a host that didn't answer isn't asked again by that
 process (each unanswered call costs the call timeout). DISPLAYS_CHANGED
 from the host already reached Wine's display-change handling, which
 enumerates again. Plain mode is unchanged.
Programs: Second Chance (an app that embeds Wine): every program shown
 below the notch of a 14" MacBook Pro (1512x945 points there, not
 1512x982).
Uncertain: Host displays are matched to real screens: the first to the
 primary, the others by overlap with the real screen's rect. Without
 EmulateModeset, the real modes are still listed and can be set, including
 ones taller than the host's display. Also without EmulateModeset: after
 the host's display changed (1512x945 to 1440x900 in the test host), Wine
 logged "No matching mode found 1512x945x32 @60" once, most likely
 restoring the test program's temporary (CDS_FULLSCREEN) mode at exit, a
 size the display no longer had; nothing visible came of it.
Evidence: tools/wmh-testhost on meeka (macOS 26.7.2, a 2560x1440 display),
 host mode, a test program printing its monitors and current mode (1 run
 each). Answering one display 0,0 1512x945 with work area 1512x920: the
 program saw monitor (0,0)-(1512,945), work (0,0)-(1512,920), current mode
 and SM_CX/CYSCREEN 1512x945, that size among the modes, and setting it
 returned DISP_CHANGE_SUCCESSFUL. After the host answered 1440x900 and sent
 DISPLAYS_CHANGED, the program saw 1440x900 throughout. Answering
 unsupported: the real display (2560x1440, work (0,30)-(2560,1351)), and
 each process asked once.
Applies to: wine 11.22 (macfixes, after 0075)
Status: 2026-11-22 (evening): host16 passed Second Chance's full hosted
 integration run on meeka (2560x1440, no notch), one run each: disc 36/36,
 download 6/6, Her downloads 3/3 (one on a rerun; the failure was in
 Second Chance's launcher), Steam 11/11, Big Fish trials 30/30, Big Fish
 owned 1/1. Its host has answered displays_get since main 62dcc9a2; which
 games asked for a display mode wasn't checked.
 2026-11-22 (later): confirmed by Second Chance on kiya (14" MacBook
 Pro, macOS 15.6.1; built-in screen 1512x982 points made main, notch strip
 37; a 2560x1440 display beside it), host16 (host15 + this patch) through
 its host (main 62dcc9a2) with no Wine helpers loaded, one run of a test
 program that opens a popup over the primary monitor: Wine saw monitor
 0,0-1512,945, work area 0,1-1512,877, current mode and SM_CX/CYSCREEN
 1512x945 at 120 Hz, and the second display at 1512,-37-4072,1403; the
 popup got 0,0-1512,945 and the host's window for it sat at Cocoa 0,0
 1512x945, filling the screen up to the notch strip. Without the helper,
 the 945 could only have come from displays_get. Not tested: a real
 display rearrangement (DISPLAYS_CHANGED) or clicks.
 2026-11-22: not yet in a build. Tested by swapping a winemac.so
 built with it into the host15 tree (see Evidence). The full suite on
 that tree: 56 passed, 0 failed, 2 skipped (the screen-taking tests).

